Progress Quest Remix - Web
==========================

This is the in-browser edition of Progress Quest, ported from Delphi
to JavaScript, remixed.

- Progress Quest Remix site:  https://enderandrew.com/ProgressQuest/
- This fork:                  https://github.com/enderandrew/ProgressQuest
- Original web version:       https://bitbucket.org/grumdrig/pq-web
- Original game:              https://bitbucket.org/grumdrig/pq


Running it
----------

Open roster.html through a web server (the game clock runs in a Web
Worker, which browsers won't start from a file:// URL). For example:

    python3 -m http.server
    # then visit http://localhost:8000/roster.html

jQuery 4 is bundled as jquery-4.0.0.min.js, so no network is needed.


Simulator
---------

sim.js runs the real game scripts headlessly in Node, finishing every
task instantly, so you can watch a character grow to level 50 in a few
seconds. Runs with the same seed are identical.

    node sim.js --levels 50 --seed pq
    node sim.js --race "4chan Troll" --class "Barbarian Pretzel" --quiet
    node sim.js --help

Use it to check that a change behaves the way you expect before playing.


Save format
-----------

Saves carry a saveVersion number (SaveVersion in config.js). When a
change needs existing saves patched, bump SaveVersion and add a
function to SaveMigrations; old saves are upgraded once when they load.


Attribute profiles
------------------

Every race and class names a primary and a secondary attribute
("4chan Troll|STR,INT"). Together they give each character a profile:
the primary counts 2, the secondary 1, and an attribute named by both
race and class counts twice. On the character sheet, primaries get a
filled star and secondaries an open one.

- Profile attributes are more likely to go up when a stat is won
  (K.StatPickBase in config.js controls how much more).
- HP Max and MP Max in the profile grow 15% faster per point of weight
  on level-up (K.PoolGrowthPerWeight).
- The martial/arcane split (STR, CON, DEX, HP Max vs INT, WIS, CHA,
  MP Max) is shown under the stats; later it decides how often a
  character casts spells.
